Unofficial Valgrind fork maintained by the hotpath-rs project
=============================================================

This is NOT an official Valgrind release and is not affiliated with or
endorsed by the Valgrind project. The official sources are at
https://sourceware.org/git/valgrind.git and https://valgrind.org/.

Fork: https://github.com/hotpath-rs/valgrind
Base: upstream tag VALGRIND_3_28_0 (Valgrind 3.28.0)
Version string: 3.28.0.hotpath-rs-2 (`valgrind --version`, Callgrind `creator:`)
Release tag: VALGRIND_3_28_0_HOTPATH_RS_2, never moved.

Every release that changes instruction counts gets a new version string
and tag (3.28.0.hotpath-rs-<n>, VALGRIND_3_28_0_HOTPATH_RS_<n>), so reports
from different builds are never compared with each other. Earlier tags stay
published as the complete corresponding source of their binaries.


License
-------

This fork does not change Valgrind's licensing. Valgrind is licensed
under the GNU General Public License, version 3 (see COPYING and the
README). The notice at the top of each file governs that file: most
allow GPL version 3 or any later version, some carry other terms (for
example the client headers include/valgrind.h.in and
callgrind/callgrind.h use a BSD-style license, and some imported files
are GPL version 2 or later). The documentation is covered by the GNU
Free Documentation License (COPYING.DOCS).

All original copyright and license notices are kept unchanged. The
hotpath-rs project claims no ownership of upstream code. Its
modifications, listed below, are contributed under the GNU General
Public License, version 3 or (at your option) any later version, and
each modified file carries a dated notice.


Distribution
------------

Every binary archive built from this fork contains COPYING, COPYING.DOCS
and README.hotpath, and names the release tag whose commit is its
complete corresponding source. Source archives made with `make dist`
include the same files. Release binaries are built and published by
.github/workflows/release.yml from the tagged commit.


Modifications
-------------

2026-11-01  VEX/priv/guest_arm64_toIR.c (arm64 guest, "B{L} uncond")

  An unconditional immediate B (without link) was given jump kind
  Ijk_Call, the same as BL. Callgrind therefore treated every plain B
  as a function call: each intra-function branch pushed a call frame,
  frames piled up (in loops, millions deep) until a real return unwound
  them, and inclusive costs overlapped. Symptoms: spurious recursion
  (`fn'2`) in non-recursive functions, including glibc's ld.so, and
  inclusive costs far above the program total. Exclusive (self) costs
  were not affected.

  Fix: B gets Ijk_Boring, BL keeps Ijk_Call. A B to the start of
  another function (a tail call) is still recognised by Callgrind's own
  jump-to-function-entry handling.

  Possibly related upstream report:
  https://bugs.kde.org/show_bug.cgi?id=472460

2026-11-02  configure.ac, .github/workflows/release.yml

  Version suffix `hotpath-rs-2` (tag VALGRIND_3_28_0_HOTPATH_RS_2) for
  the release carrying the callgrind/bbcc.c fix below: it changes arm64
  inclusive counts, so its reports must not be compared with those of
  3.28.0.hotpath-rs. The release workflow accepts the hotpath-rs-<n>
  suffix and its matching tag.

2026-11-02  callgrind/bbcc.c (setup_bbcc, RET handling, arm64 only)

  On arm64 a call keeps its return address in x30, so after a RET the
  SP equals the SP Callgrind recorded for the returning frame (on x86
  the call pushed the return address, so the frame lies strictly below
  the SP after the RET and is always unwound). A RET is granted one pop
  for frames at the same SP. When another frame sat above the returning
  one, that pop was spent on it and the returning frame stayed open.
  The usual source is "bl foo@plt": the skipped PLT stub's frame and
  foo's frame share the SP and the return address, and foo's RET popped
  only foo's frame. The stale frame then made the caller's RET leave the
  caller open too. From then on, every later call nested inside the
  open frames, and the open frames' inclusive costs kept growing until
  the thread unwound past them. Symptoms: spurious recursion (`fn'2`)
  between unrelated sibling calls, and inclusive costs per function far
  above the program total. Exclusive (self) costs were not affected.

  Fix: on a RET, Callgrind looks down the frames at or below the
  current SP for the one whose SP equals the current SP and whose
  return address is the RET target. It pops through that frame and
  through any frames under it recorded for the same call (same SP and
  return address). If no frame matches, the upstream logic applies
  unchanged. Other architectures are not affected.

2026-11-01  configure.ac

  Version suffix `hotpath-rs` (tag VALGRIND_3_28_0_HOTPATH_RS), so reports
  from this build are never mistaken for, or compared with, an official
  3.28.0 build.

2026-11-01  README.hotpath, Makefile.am

  License section describing Valgrind's mixed licensing precisely;
  README.hotpath added to EXTRA_DIST so source archives carry it.


Building
--------

  ./autogen.sh
  ./configure --prefix=<install dir>
  make -j"$(nproc)"
  make install
